    "D",
    "E",
    "F",
    "G"
  ],
  "correct": "D",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Bound the possible inputs",
      "body": "<p>The square root requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>cos</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, equality also requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "Only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math> can contain solutions.",
      "pitfall": "Cosine’s infinitely many later oscillations cannot produce further roots.",
      "pause": "Where is cosine nonnegative inside this interval?",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l07",
          "reminder": "Use the exact range of an inner function to restrict a composite equation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Keep only positive cosine arches",
      "body": "<p>Possible intersections lie in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. On intervals where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the two sides cannot agree.</p>",
      "recap": "Only two nonnegative cosine pieces remain.",
      "pitfall": "Angles are in radians, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> itself is not a solution.",
      "pause": "Compare the two curves at 0, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l15",
          "reminder": "Use cosine’s signs, period and exact landmark values in the stated angle units."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Count crossings, not exact roots",
      "body": "<p>On each retained cosine piece, compare the curves at its endpoints and, for a complete arch, at its peak. Use the shapes between those landmarks to decide where intersections occur.</p>",
      "recap": "Root counting needs the relative positions and shapes of both curves on the allowed intervals.",
      "pitfall": "Touching and crossing each count as one solution; do not count landmarks unless equality holds.",
      "pause": "Complete a labelled sketch and count every permitted intersection.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-graphical-solutions",
          "reminder": "Use key heights and graph shape to count intersections without finding the roots explicitly."
